Add a WAL redo worker mode to postgres.

Materializing a page at some LSN means replaying WAL records onto an
older page image. Reimplementing every rmgr redo routine outside
postgres is a losing game, the heap and btree routines alone encode
years of subtle decisions, so instead the page service runs the real
routines inside postgres itself. This is the approach Neon proved
out with their walredo process. A worker starts as
"postgres --zou-redo <scratchdir> [checksum_version]" and speaks a
small binary protocol on stdin and stdout: push base page images,
push WAL records, ask for pages back. Pages live in a private
in-memory smgr wired in as a third smgrsw entry, so nothing ever
touches a data directory, and a fabricated control file replaces
pg_control. On Linux the worker locks itself down with seccomp
before reading the first message, since it processes page bytes that
ultimately come from tenants.

The sandbox and the stats system disagree about DSM. Before every
buffered read AsyncReadBuffers prepares a checksum failure report,
and preparing one means creating a stats entry ref for the database,
which can grow the shared stats arena by a fresh segment. dsm_create
calls shm_open, the seccomp filter kills the worker on the spot, and
the pool only sees a dead pipe. Whether the entry fits an already
mapped segment depends on allocator state, so a store that lines up
the misses fails its recovery every single time while everything
else sails through. The worker's pages are scratch, their stats mean
nothing, so both the preparation and the report skip out early under
zou_redo_active.

Records carry an explicit end LSN instead of letting the worker
compute lsn plus the aligned record length. Redo routines stamp
EndRecPtr into pages as the page LSN, and when a record crosses a
WAL page boundary the true end includes the next page's header, so
the computed value would be short and materialized pages would
disagree with the primary by exactly a page header size.

The checksum_version argument must match the origin cluster. Redo
behavior branches on XLogHintBitIsNeeded, heap_xlog_visible for one
only advances the page LSN when checksums are on, and with a nonzero
version the worker verifies checksums on every page it is handed, so
base images pushed to it must carry valid ones. The pool recomputes
checksums on pages it serves out, since replay legitimately changes
page contents without updating pd_checksum.

Records from custom rmgrs error out for now. Supporting extensions
that install their own rmgrs means loading their libraries into the
worker, which is future work.

The zou_smgr.h include in ipci.c rides along here. The zou shmem
calls were picked up through an implicit declaration before, which
newer clang treats as an error.

